# What must never reach the build context.
#
# CI deploys `--remote-only` from a clean checkout, so the images it builds are
# already fine. This file is for the other case: `fly deploy` run from a working
# copy, which carries things a clean checkout does not. A file copied into a
# layer stays in that layer even if a later one deletes it, so an image built
# from a developer's checkout can carry a password that appears nowhere in the
# filesystem that image produces.
#
# Being in .gitignore is not being in this file. Git and Docker read different
# lists, and most of what is added below is precisely what git already ignores
# -- which is why it is sitting on disk to be copied.

node_modules
dist
.git
.vercel

# Worktrees: hundreds of megabytes of other branches, and each one is a second
# copy of the repository carrying everything else named in this file.
.worktrees

# Real credentials. secrets/ holds the owner password in plain text on this
# machine right now. It is the reason this file was revisited.
secrets

# Agent state. The transcripts and conversations are working notes that quote
# file contents back at length, and .claude is session configuration. None of it
# is the product, and none of it should travel to whoever can pull an image.
.claude
.forge/transcripts
.forge/conversations

# The walk output: supplier-run screenshots, large and nobody's business but ours.
.forge/shots

# Local databases and anything shaped like one. The real database lives on the
# mounted volume; a copy baked into the image would be stale customer records
# travelling with the release.
backend/data
*.sqlite*

*.log
.env*

# Deliberately NOT ignored: src/data/. backend/server.mjs reads
# scraped-tires.json from there at boot to seed the catalog, and the frontend
# imports catalog.js from the same directory. Measured, not guessed: a rule
# matching the whole directory fails the image build with "Module not found",
# and a rule matching only the JSON builds an image that then dies at boot with
# ENOENT. Neither is the quiet empty-catalog this comment first claimed -- that
# shape belongs to a file read AFTER boot, like backend/zip-centroids.json,
# where the image starts and serves and only fails when a customer touches the
# feature. If you ever add a data-shaped rule here, name the directory exactly.
